(Anisotropic filtering matters here more than raw resolution. A page held at an angle presents a) Tj T*
(strongly foreshortened surface, and isotropic mipmapping will select a level appropriate to the) Tj T*
(compressed axis, blurring the axis that is still well sampled. Turning anisotropy up to the) Tj T*
(hardware maximum is nearly free and recovers most of the apparent sharpness.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Curvature near the spine is where the illusion is won or lost. A perfectly flat page reads as a) Tj T*
(poster. A page that bows into the gutter, catching light along the fold and compressing the) Tj T*
(innermost characters slightly, reads as paper. The compression must stay small: a few degrees of) Tj T*
(tangent deviation is enough to be felt and not enough to be resented.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(None of this is settled by argument. Build the smallest possible thing that puts real type on a) Tj T*
(real curved surface at a real reading distance, then look at it. If the text can be read without) Tj T*
(leaning in, the idea survives. If it cannot, no amount of architecture downstream will rescue it,) Tj T*
(and the honest move is to flatten the page and keep the room.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A library is not a warehouse of finished thoughts but an instrument for producing new ones. The) Tj T*
(shelf is the argument: what stands beside what, which spine catches the eye on the way to) Tj T*
(somewhere else, which book has been pulled so often that it no longer sits flush with its) Tj T*
(neighbours. Cataloguing systems try to freeze this into an order, and every reader quietly) Tj T*
(subverts it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
